---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>What to Do in a Spousal Support Dispute</h1>
  <p>If you're heading into a divorce where spousal support will be contested, the decisions you make in the first few months matter for years.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: Get a family-law attorney (or at least consult one)</h2>
  <p>For any significant spousal support claim, the cost-benefit favors getting a lawyer. Most offer free or low-cost consultations. Even one meeting will tell you:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your state's framework and likely range of outcomes</li>
    <li>Whether your case favors formula or discretionary analysis</li>
    <li>Approximate timeline and cost</li>
    <li>Whether mediation makes sense</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 2: Document everything</h2>
  <p>Spousal support cases are heavily fact-dependent. Start collecting:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>3 years of joint tax returns</li>
    <li>Recent pay stubs (both spouses if accessible)</li>
    <li>Bank statements showing standard of living</li>
    <li>Records of non-monetary contributions (homemaking, supporting the other's career)</li>
    <li>Documentation of any health issues or earning-capacity changes</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 3: Get the income story straight</h2>
  <p>If you're the lower-earning spouse, you want full disclosure of the other's actual income (not just W-2). For self-employed spouses, deferred compensation, bonuses, stock options, and pass-through entity income can significantly affect support calculations.</p>
  <p>Forensic accounting may be warranted for high-asset cases.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: Consider mediation</h2>
  <p>Most family-law cases settle without trial. Mediation costs less, moves faster, and often produces better outcomes than litigation &mdash; especially when both spouses can be reasonable.</p>
  <p>Family-law mediators can help structure a support framework that accommodates both parties' needs and preferences (e.g., front-loading payments, lump-sum buyouts).</p>

  <h2>Step 5: Plan for tax implications</h2>
  <p>For US divorces finalized after 12/31/2018: spousal support is NOT deductible to payer and NOT taxable to recipient. This means the payer typically wants smaller amounts (paying with after-tax dollars), and the recipient receives more after-tax money than under the old rules. Negotiate with these tax facts in mind.</p>

  <h2>Step 6: Consider duration vs. amount tradeoffs</h2>
  <p>Sometimes a higher amount for shorter duration is better than a lower amount for longer duration, or vice versa. Lump-sum buyouts can also be an option that gives certainty to both parties.</p>

  <h2>Step 7: If you go to trial</h2>
  <p>Trials are expensive, slow, and uncertain. Most cases settle before trial because the cost-benefit doesn't work. If you do go to trial:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Make sure your evidence is well-organized</li>
    <li>Prepare for the judge's discretion to override formulas</li>
    <li>Consider vocational experts to establish earning capacity</li>
    <li>Be realistic about outcome ranges &mdash; "winning" is rarely a clean answer in family court</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What NOT to do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don't hide income or assets.</strong> Discovery is thorough; getting caught is worse than disclosure.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't quit your job to avoid support.</strong> Courts impute income when this is obvious.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't move out without legal advice.</strong> Can affect custody and property arguments.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't agree to terms verbally before a written agreement.</strong> Family-law agreements need to be in writing.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't use children as leverage.</strong> Counterproductive and may damage custody position.</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
